Here's an example of how you can implement the program using JavaScript:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>HTTP Request</title>
  <style>
    .error {
      color: red;
      font-weight: bold;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>HTTP Request</h1>

  <label for="urlInput">Enter API URL:</label>
  <input type="text" id="urlInput">
  <button onclick="makeRequest()">Make Request</button>

  <div id="response"></div>
  <div id="error" class="error"></div>

  <script>
    const responseContainer = document.getElementById("response");
    const errorContainer = document.getElementById("error");

    function validateUrl(url) {
      try {
        new URL(url);
        return true;
      } catch (_) {
        return false;
      }
    }

    function displayResponse(response) {
      responseContainer.innerHTML = JSON.stringify(response, null, 2);
    }

    function displayError(error) {
      errorContainer.innerHTML = error.message;
    }

    function makeRequest() {
      const urlInput = document.getElementById("urlInput");
      const url = urlInput.value;

      if (!validateUrl(url)) {
        displayError({ message: "Invalid URL" });
        return;
      }

      fetch(url)
        .then(response => {
          if (!response.ok) {
            throw new Error(`HTTP error! Status: ${response.status}`);
          }

          return response.json();
        })
        .then(responseData => {
          displayResponse(responseData);
        })
        .catch(error => {
          displayError(error);
        });
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

This program sets up a basic HTML page with an input field and a button for the user to enter the API URL and make the request. The response data will be displayed in a `<div>` element with the id "response", and any errors will be displayed in a separate `<div>` element with the id "error".

The `validateUrl` function is used to validate the user input to ensure it is a valid URL. It uses the `URL` constructor to check if the input can be parsed as a valid URL.

The `makeRequest` function is triggered when the user clicks the "Make Request" button. It retrieves the URL entered by the user, validates it, and then uses the Fetch API to make the HTTP request. If the response is not successful (i.e., has a status other than 2xx), an error is thrown. Otherwise, the response data is converted to JSON and displayed on the webpage.

The `displayResponse` and `displayError` functions are used to update the corresponding `<div>` elements with the response data or error message.

