### Instruction:
Explain with a code snippet how to execute an AJAX request in JavaScript and display the response data in a table format on a webpage.

### Response:
Sure! Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates how to execute an AJAX request in JavaScript and display the response data in a table format on a webpage:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>AJAX Request Example</title>
</head>
<body>
  <button onclick="loadData()">Load Data</button>
  <table id="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>ID</th>
        <th>Name</th>
        <th>Email</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody id="data-body">
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <script>
    function loadData() {
      // Create an XMLHttpRequest object
      var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();

      // Define the AJAX request
      xhr.open('GET', 'data.json', true);

      // Set the response type
      xhr.responseType = 'json';

      // Define the callback function
      xhr.onload = function() {
        if (xhr.status === 200) {
          var data = xhr.response;

          // Get the table body element
          var tableBody = document.getElementById('data-body');

          // Clear the existing table rows
          tableBody.innerHTML = '';

          // Loop through the data and create table rows
          for (var i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
            var row = document.createElement('tr');
            row.innerHTML = '<td>' + data[i].id + '</td>' +
                            '<td>' + data[i].name + '</td>' +
                            '<td>' + data[i].email + '</td>';
            tableBody.appendChild(row);
          }
        }
      };

      // Send the AJAX request
      xhr.send();
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example, we have a button element with an `onclick` event listener that triggers the `loadData()` function. Inside this function, we create an XMLHttpRequest object and define the AJAX request using the `open()` method. We set the response type to `json` and define the `onload` callback function that will be executed when the request is successful.

Inside the callback function, we check if the response status is 200 (indicating success). If so, we retrieve the response data, which is an array of objects, and loop through it to create table rows dynamically. We clear the existing table rows by emptying the `innerHTML` of the table body element. Then, for each object in the data array, we create a new table row using `createElement()` and set its innerHTML with the object properties (id, name, and email). Finally, we append the newly created row to the table body.

Note that in this example, the data is retrieved from a `data.json` file. You can modify the URL in the `open()` method to fetch data from a different source (e.g., an API endpoint).

