Create a React web page with two app components that fetches data from an API and displays it in a table. The page should also have a search functionality that allows users to filter the data based on specific criteria.

### Response:
Sure! Here's an example of how you can create a React web page with two app components that fetch data from an API and display it in a table. The page also includes a search functionality that allows users to filter the data based on specific criteria.

First, make sure you have installed React and axios (a popular library for making HTTP requests) by running the following command in your terminal:
```
npm install react axios
```

Then, create a new file called `App.js` and add the following code:

```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import axios from 'axios';

const App = () => {
  const [data, setData] = useState([]);
  const [searchTerm, setSearchTerm] = useState('');

  useEffect(() => {
    fetchData();
  }, []);

  const fetchData = async () => {
    try {
      const response = await axios.get('https://api.example.com/data');
      setData(response.data);
    } catch (error) {
      console.log(error);
    }
  };

  const handleSearch = (event) => {
    setSearchTerm(event.target.value);
  };

  const filteredData = data.filter((item) =>
    item.name.toLowerCase().includes(searchTerm.toLowerCase())
  );

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        type="text"
        placeholder="Search by name..."
        value={searchTerm}
        onChange={handleSearch}
      />
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Name</th>
            <th>Email</th>
            <th>Phone</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {filteredData.map((item) => (
            <tr key={item.id}>
              <td>{item.name}</td>
              <td>{item.email}</td>
              <td>{item.phone}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```

In this example, we create a functional component called `App`. Inside the component, we use the `useState` hook to manage the state of `data` (the fetched data) and `searchTerm` (the search input value). We use the `useEffect` hook to fetch the data from the API when the component mounts.

The `fetchData` function uses axios to make an HTTP GET request to the API endpoint and sets the fetched data to the `data` state variable using the `setData` function.

The `handleSearch` function updates the `searchTerm` state variable when the search input value changes.

We filter the data based on the `searchTerm` using the `filter` method. The filtered data is stored in the `filteredData` variable.

Finally, we render an input element for the search functionality and a table that displays the filtered data.

Make sure to replace `'https://api.example.com/data'` with the actual API endpoint URL.

To use the `App` component, create a new file called `index.js` and add the following code:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';
import App from './App';

ReactDOM.render(<App />, document.getElementById('root'));
```

This will render the `App` component and mount it in the HTML element with the ID 'root'.

That's it! You can now run your React web page by running the following command in your terminal:
```
npm start
```

Your React web page with two app components that fetches data from an API and displays it in a table, along with search functionality, should be up and running.

