Create an API in Go that takes a string and returns the amount of words present in the given string. However, the API should also be able to handle large strings with millions of words efficiently. Ensure that the solution can handle strings with a maximum length of 1 GB and has a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(1), where n is the length of the input string.

### Response:
To handle large strings efficiently with a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(1), we can iterate over the string character by character and count the number of words. We will define a word as a sequence of non-whitespace characters.

Here's an example implementation of the API in Go:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"net/http"
	"strings"
)

func wordCountHandler(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
	if r.Method != http.MethodPost {
		w.WriteHeader(http.StatusMethodNotAllowed)
		return
	}

	data := make([]byte, r.ContentLength)
	_, err := r.Body.Read(data)
	if err != nil {
		w.WriteHeader(http.StatusInternalServerError)
		return
	}

	wordCount := countWords(string(data))
	fmt.Fprintf(w, "Word count: %d\n", wordCount)
}

func countWords(s string) int {
	wordCount := 0
	inWord := false

	for _, c := range s {
		if c == ' ' || c == '\t' || c == '\n' || c == '\r' {
			inWord = false
		} else if !inWord {
			wordCount++
			inWord = true
		}
	}

	return wordCount
}

func main() {
	http.HandleFunc("/wordcount", wordCountHandler)
	http.ListenAndServe(":8080", nil)
}
```

This code sets up an HTTP server that listens on port 8080 and handles POST requests to the "/wordcount" endpoint. The request body should contain the string to be counted.

The `wordCountHandler` function reads the request body and passes the string to the `countWords` function. The `countWords` function iterates over each character in the string, keeping track of whether it is currently inside a word or not. If it encounters whitespace characters, it marks the end of a word and increments the count. Finally, it returns the word count.

Please note that in the above implementation, the `countWords` function assumes that the string does not contain any Unicode characters that require more than one byte to represent. If your input strings can contain such characters, you will need to modify the function accordingly to correctly handle Unicode encoding.

