First, make sure you have the necessary dependencies installed by running `npm install express` and `npm install express-rate-limit`. Then, create a new file called `app.js` and add the following code:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');
const fs = require('fs');
const app = express();

// Create a rate limiter that allows only 5 requests per minute from the same IP address
const limiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 1 * 60 * 1000, // 1 minute
  max: 5
});

// Apply the rate limiter to all requests
app.use(limiter);

// Create a custom log file for logging all incoming requests
const accessLogStream = fs.createWriteStream('access.log', { flags: 'a' });

// Use the logger for all incoming requests
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  accessLogStream.write(`${req.ip} - ${req.method} ${req.originalUrl}\n`);
  next();
});

// Calculate the Fibonacci sequence up to a given number
const fibonacci = (n) => {
  const sequence = [0, 1];

  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    const num = sequence[i - 1] + sequence[i - 2];
    sequence.push(num);
  }

  return sequence;
};

// Define the API endpoint
app.get('/fibonacci/:number', (req, res) => {
  const number = parseInt(req.params.number);
  const sequence = fibonacci(number);

  res.json({ sequence });
});

// Start the server
app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server started on port 3000');
});
```

In this example, we use the Express framework to handle incoming requests. The `rateLimit` middleware from the `express-rate-limit` package is used to implement rate limiting. We create a rate limiter that allows a maximum of 5 requests per minute from the same IP address. The `limiter` is then applied to all requests using `app.use(limiter)`.

We also create a custom log file using the `fs` module and write all incoming requests to it. The `accessLogStream` is created with the `'a'` flag to append new logs to the existing file.

The `/fibonacci/:number` endpoint is defined to accept a positive integer as a parameter. The `fibonacci` function calculates the Fibonacci sequence up to that number. The result is then returned as a JSON response.

Finally, we start the server on port 3000 using `app.listen(3000, ...)`. You can change the port number as per your preference.

To run the application, execute `node app.js` in the terminal. You can then make requests to `http://localhost:3000/fibonacci/:number`, replacing `:number` with the desired positive integer, to get the Fibonacci sequence up to that number. The access logs will be stored in the `access.log` file.

