### Instruction:
Create a React component that displays a grid layout of items. Each item should have the following properties:
- id: a unique identifier for the item (string)
- name: the name of the item (string)
- price: the price of the item (number)
- imageURL: the URL of the image representing the item (string)

The component should fetch the list of items from an API endpoint and display them in a grid layout. Each item should be displayed with its name, price, and image. When an item is clicked, it should be highlighted and its details (name, price, and image) should be displayed in a separate section of the page.

The component should have the following additional features:
1. Pagination: The component should display a maximum of 10 items per page. It should provide buttons to navigate to the next and previous pages of items. The user should also be able to jump to a specific page by entering a page number.
2. Sorting: The component should allow the user to sort the items by name or price in ascending or descending order. The user should be able to specify the sorting order for each property separately.
3. Filtering: The component should allow the user to filter the items by name or price range. The user should be able to specify a minimum and maximum price to filter the items. Additionally, the user should be able to filter the items by multiple names simultaneously.

Note: You can use any libraries or frameworks you prefer to implement the component.

### Response:
Here's an example implementation of the React component with the requested features:

```jsx
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const ItemGrid = () => {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);
  const [selectedItem, setSelectedItem] = useState(null);
  const [currentPage, setCurrentPage] = useState(1);
  const [totalPages, setTotalPages] = useState(0);
  const [sortBy, setSortBy] = useState('name');
  const [sortOrder, setSortOrder] = useState('asc');
  const [nameFilter, setNameFilter] = useState('');
  const [minPriceFilter, setMinPriceFilter] = useState('');
  const [maxPriceFilter, setMaxPriceFilter] = useState('');

  useEffect(() => {
    fetchItems();
  }, [currentPage, sortBy, sortOrder, nameFilter, minPriceFilter, maxPriceFilter]);

  const fetchItems = async () => {
    // Construct the API URL with query parameters
    let url = `https://api.example.com/items?page=${currentPage}&sort_by=${sortBy}&sort_order=${sortOrder}`;

    if (nameFilter) {
      url += `&name=${nameFilter}`;
    }

    if (minPriceFilter) {
      url += `&min_price=${minPriceFilter}`;
    }

    if (maxPriceFilter) {
      url += `&max_price=${maxPriceFilter}`;
    }

    try {
      const response = await fetch(url);
      const data = await response.json();
      setItems(data.items);
      setTotalPages(data.totalPages);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error fetching items:', error);
    }
  };

  const handleItemSelect = (item) => {
    setSelectedItem(item);
  };

  const handlePageChange = (page) => {
    setCurrentPage(page);
  };

  const handleSortByChange = (event) => {
    setSortBy(event.target.value);
  };

  const handleSortOrderChange = (event) => {
    setSortOrder(event.target.value);
  };

  const handleNameFilterChange = (event) => {
    setNameFilter(event.target.value);
  };

  const handleMinPriceFilterChange = (event) => {
    setMinPriceFilter(event.target.value);
  };

  const handleMaxPriceFilterChange = (event) => {
    setMaxPriceFilter(event.target.value);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <div>
        <label>Sort by:</label>
        <select value={sortBy} onChange={handleSortByChange}>
          <option value="name">Name</option>
          <option value="price">Price</option>
        </select>
        <select value={sortOrder} onChange={handleSortOrderChange}>
          <option value="asc">Ascending</option>
          <option value="desc">Descending</option>
        </select>
      </div>
      <div>
        <label>Name filter:</label>
        <input type="text" value={nameFilter} onChange={handleNameFilterChange} />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label>Min price filter:</label>
        <input type="number" value={minPriceFilter} onChange={handleMinPriceFilterChange} />
      </div>
      <div>
        <label>Max price filter:</label>
        <input type="number" value={maxPriceFilter} onChange={handleMaxPriceFilterChange} />
      </div>
      <div>
        {items.map((item) => (
          <div
            key={item.id}
            onClick={() => handleItemSelect(item)}
            style={{
              border: '1px solid',
              padding: '10px',
              cursor: 'pointer',
              backgroundColor: selectedItem?.id === item.id ? 'lightblue' : 'white',
            }}
          >
            <img src={item.imageURL} alt={item.name} style={{ width: '100px' }} />
            <p>{item.name}</p>
            <p>{item.price}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div>
        <button disabled={currentPage === 1} onClick={() => handlePageChange(currentPage - 1)}>
          Previous
        </button>
        <button disabled={currentPage === totalPages} onClick={() => handlePageChange(currentPage + 1)}>
          Next
        </button>
        <input
          type="number"
          min="1"
          max={totalPages}
          value={currentPage}
          onChange={(event) => handlePageChange(Number(event.target.value))}
        />
      </div>
      {selectedItem && (
        <div>
          <h2>Selected item:</h2>
          <img src={selectedItem.imageURL} alt={selectedItem.name} style={{ width: '100px' }} />
          <p>{selectedItem.name}</p>
          <p>{selectedItem.price}</p>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  );
};

export default ItemGrid;
```

In this implementation, the component fetches the list of items from the API endpoint based on the current page, sorting options, and filters. The fetched items are then rendered in a grid layout with their details. The user can interact with the sorting options, filters, and pagination buttons to update the displayed items.

Please note that this is a simplified example and may require modifications based on your specific use case and API endpoint structure.

