### Instruction:
Write a Java web service that takes in an input string and returns the string "Hello world" if the input is a palindrome. The web service should handle multiple concurrent requests and should be able to handle large input strings efficiently.

In addition to the given requirements, the web service should also validate the input string to ensure it contains only alphanumeric characters and ignore any special characters or whitespace. If the input contains any invalid characters, the web service should return an appropriate error message.

Furthermore, the web service should implement a caching mechanism to store previously processed input strings and their corresponding results. The caching mechanism should be efficient and should not impact the performance of the web service.

Finally, the web service should be able to handle a high volume of concurrent requests without experiencing any performance degradation. It should be scalable and designed to handle heavy traffic efficiently.

### Response:
Here is an example implementation of a Java web service that meets the given requirements:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.EnableCaching;
import org.springframework.cache.annotation.Cacheable;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

@SpringBootApplication
@EnableCaching
public class PalindromeWebServiceApplication {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(PalindromeWebServiceApplication.class, args);
    }
}

@RestController
class PalindromeController {

    private final PalindromeService palindromeService;

    PalindromeController(PalindromeService palindromeService) {
        this.palindromeService = palindromeService;
    }

    @GetMapping("/palindrome/{input}")
    public String checkPalindrome(@PathVariable String input) {
        return palindromeService.checkIfPalindrome(input);
    }
}

@Service
class PalindromeService {

    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public String checkIfPalindrome(String input) {
        // Remove special characters and whitespace
        String cleanInput = input.replaceAll("[^a-zA-Z0-9]", "");

        if (cleanInput.isEmpty()) {
            return "Error: Invalid input";
        }

        // Check if already processed
        if (cache.containsKey(cleanInput)) {
            return cache.get(cleanInput);
        }

        String result = isPalindrome(cleanInput) ? "Hello world" : "Not a palindrome";

        // Cache the result
        cache.put(cleanInput, result);

        return result;
    }

    @Cacheable(value = "palindromes")
    public boolean isPalindrome(String input) {
        int i = 0, j = input.length() - 1;

        while (i < j) {
            if (input.charAt(i) != input.charAt(j)) {
                return false;
            }
            i++;
            j--;
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

This implementation uses Spring Boot and Spring Caching to simplify the development of the web service. The `PalindromeWebServiceApplication` class is the main entry point of the application.

The `PalindromeController` class is a REST controller that exposes a GET endpoint at `/palindrome/{input}`. It injects the `PalindromeService` to perform the palindrome check.

The `PalindromeService` class is responsible for validating the input, checking if it is a palindrome, and caching the results. The `checkIfPalindrome` method removes special characters and whitespace from the input, checks if the input is already cached, and returns the cached result if available. Otherwise, it checks if the input is a palindrome using the `isPalindrome` method. If it is a palindrome, it returns "Hello world" and caches the result. If it is not a palindrome, it returns "Not a palindrome".

The `isPalindrome` method uses the `@Cacheable` annotation to cache the results of the palindrome check. This ensures that subsequent requests with the same input will return the cached result instead of recalculating it.

Note that this implementation uses an in-memory cache (`ConcurrentHashMap`) for simplicity. In a production environment, you might consider using a distributed cache like Redis for better scalability and performance.

To run the web service, you can use the `SpringApplication.run` method in the `main` method of the `PalindromeWebServiceApplication` class. The web service will be accessible at `http://localhost:8080/palindrome/{input}`.

