### Instruction:
Create a Node.js server that serves up a webpage when a GET request is received. The webpage should be dynamically generated and include real-time data from an external API. The server should also implement user authentication using JWT tokens.

### Response:
Sure! Here's an example of how you can create a Node.js server that serves up a webpage with real-time data from an external API and implements user authentication using JWT tokens:

First, make sure you have Node.js installed on your machine. Then, create a new directory for your project and navigate to it in your terminal. Initialize a new Node.js project by running the following command:

```
npm init -y
```

Next, install the required dependencies:

```
npm install express axios jsonwebtoken dotenv
```

Now, create a new file called `server.js` and open it in your preferred code editor. Copy and paste the following code into `server.js`:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const axios = require('axios');
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');
require('dotenv').config();

const app = express();
app.use(express.json());

const secretKey = process.env.JWT_SECRET_KEY;

// Authenticate user and generate JWT token
app.post('/login', (req, res) => {
  // Check if the username and password are valid
  // In a real application, this would involve querying a database
  const { username, password } = req.body;
  if (username === 'admin' && password === 'admin') {
    // Generate and send JWT token
    const token = jwt.sign({ username }, secretKey);
    res.json({ token });
  } else {
    res.status(401).json({ message: 'Invalid username or password' });
  }
});

// Middleware to authenticate user
const authenticateUser = (req, res, next) => {
  const authHeader = req.headers['authorization'];
  const token = authHeader && authHeader.split(' ')[1];
  if (token == null) return res.sendStatus(401);

  jwt.verify(token, secretKey, (err, user) => {
    if (err) return res.sendStatus(403);
    req.user = user;
    next();
  });
};

// Fetch data from external API and return it
const fetchData = async () => {
  try {
    const response = await axios.get('https://api.example.com/data');
    return response.data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching data:', error);
    return null;
  }
};

// Serve up the webpage with real-time data
app.get('/', authenticateUser, async (req, res) => {
  const data = await fetchData();
  if (data) {
    res.send(`
      <html>
        <body>
          <h1>Welcome, ${req.user.username}!</h1>
          <p>Data from external API: ${data}</p>
        </body>
      </html>
    `);
  } else {
    res.status(500).send('Internal Server Error');
  }
});

// Start the server
const port = process.env.PORT || 3000;
app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Server is running on port ${port}`);
});
```

In the above code, we use the Express framework to create a server. The server listens for a POST request to the `/login` endpoint to authenticate the user and generate a JWT token. It also listens for a GET request to the root endpoint `/`, but only allows access to authenticated users by using the `authenticateUser` middleware.

The server makes use of the `axios` library to fetch data from an external API. In this example, the data is simply displayed on the webpage.

Make sure you have a `.env` file in your project directory with the `JWT_SECRET_KEY` value set. For example:

```
JWT_SECRET_KEY=your-secret-key
```

To start the server, run the following command in your terminal:

```
node server.js
```

Now, when you navigate to `http://localhost:3000` in your browser, you should see a login page. You can log in with the username `admin` and password `admin` (as specified in the `/login` endpoint), and you'll be redirected to the main page with the dynamically generated content from the external API.

Note: In a real application, you would need to replace the `fetchData` function with your actual code to fetch data from the external API and modify the authentication logic according to your needs.

