### Instruction:
Create a React component to render a table with data from an API. The API returns an array of objects, each representing a person's information. Each person has the following properties: name (a string), age (a number), and job (a string).

The table should display the following columns: Name, Age, and Job. The data from the API should be fetched and displayed in the table.

Additionally, the table should have the following features:
1. Sorting: The user should be able to click on the column headers to sort the table by that column in ascending or descending order.
2. Filtering: The user should be able to enter a search term in a search input field, and the table should update dynamically to display only the rows that match the search term. The search should be case-insensitive and should match the search term against all columns.
3. Pagination: The table should display a maximum of 10 rows per page. If there are more than 10 rows in the data, the table should display pagination buttons to navigate between pages.

The component should be reusable and should accept the data from the API as a prop. The component should also emit events when the user interacts with the sorting, filtering, and pagination features, so that the parent component can handle the corresponding logic.

### Response:
Here is an example of a React component that meets the requirements outlined:

```jsx
import React, { useState } from "react";

const Table = ({ data }) => {
  const [currentPage, setCurrentPage] = useState(1);
  const [searchTerm, setSearchTerm] = useState("");
  const [sortConfig, setSortConfig] = useState({ key: null, direction: null });

  // Helper function to handle sorting
  const sortData = (key) => {
    let direction = "ascending";
    if (sortConfig.key === key && sortConfig.direction === "ascending") {
      direction = "descending";
    }
    setSortConfig({ key, direction });
  };

  // Helper function to handle pagination
  const getPageData = () => {
    const filteredData = data.filter((person) =>
      Object.values(person)
        .join(" ")
        .toLowerCase()
        .includes(searchTerm.toLowerCase())
    );
    const sortedData = filteredData.sort((a, b) => {
      if (a[sortConfig.key] < b[sortConfig.key]) {
        return sortConfig.direction === "ascending" ? -1 : 1;
      }
      if (a[sortConfig.key] > b[sortConfig.key]) {
        return sortConfig.direction === "ascending" ? 1 : -1;
      }
      return 0;
    });
    const startIndex = (currentPage - 1) * 10;
    return sortedData.slice(startIndex, startIndex + 10);
  };

  // Event handler for search input
  const handleSearch = (event) => {
    setSearchTerm(event.target.value);
    setCurrentPage(1);
  };

  // Event handler for pagination
  const handlePagination = (page) => {
    setCurrentPage(page);
  };

  const renderedData = getPageData();

  return (
    <div>
      <input
        type="text"
        placeholder="Search..."
        value={searchTerm}
        onChange={handleSearch}
      />
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th onClick={() => sortData("name")}>Name</th>
            <th onClick={() => sortData("age")}>Age</th>
            <th onClick={() => sortData("job")}>Job</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {renderedData.map((person, index) => (
            <tr key={index}>
              <td>{person.name}</td>
              <td>{person.age}</td>
              <td>{person.job}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      {data.length > 10 && (
        <div>
          {Array.from({ length: Math.ceil(data.length / 10) }).map(
            (page, index) => (
              <button
                key={index}
                onClick={() => handlePagination(index + 1)}
                disabled={currentPage === index + 1}
              >
                {index + 1}
              </button>
            )
          )}
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  );
};

export default Table;
```

