To create an API in Go that meets all the requirements mentioned, we can use a combination of regular expressions, concurrent processing, and efficient data structures. Here's an example implementation:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"net/http"
	"regexp"
	"strings"
	"sync"
)

type WordCounter struct {
	words map[string]int
	mutex sync.Mutex
}

func (wc *WordCounter) CountWords(text string) {
	// Split text into words using regular expressions
	// This handles different language-specific word delimiters and punctuation marks
	regex := regexp.MustCompile(`[\p{L}\p{N}]+`)
	words := regex.FindAllString(strings.ToLower(text), -1)

	// Count the frequency of each unique word
	for _, word := range words {
		wc.mutex.Lock()
		wc.words[word]++
		wc.mutex.Unlock()
	}
}

func (wc *WordCounter) GetUniqueWordCount() int {
	return len(wc.words)
}

func (wc *WordCounter) GetWordFrequency(word string) int {
	return wc.words[word]
}

func main() {
	wordCounter := &WordCounter{
		words: make(map[string]int),
	}

	http.HandleFunc("/wordcount", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		if r.Method != http.MethodPost {
			http.Error(w, "Method not allowed", http.StatusMethodNotAllowed)
			return
		}

		if r.ContentLength > 1<<30 { // Maximum length of 1 GB
			http.Error(w, "Request entity too large", http.StatusRequestEntityTooLarge)
			return
		}

		text := make([]byte, r.ContentLength)
		_, err := r.Body.Read(text)
		if err != nil {
			http.Error(w, "Error reading request body", http.StatusInternalServerError)
			return
		}

		wordCounter.CountWords(string(text))

		fmt.Fprintf(w, "Unique word count: %d\n", wordCounter.GetUniqueWordCount())
		fmt.Fprintf(w, "Frequency count:\n")
		for word, count := range wordCounter.words {
			fmt.Fprintf(w, "%s: %d\n", word, count)
		}

		wordCounter.mutex.Lock()
		defer wordCounter.mutex.Unlock()
		wordCounter.words = make(map[string]int) // Reset word count
	})

	http.ListenAndServe(":8080", nil)
}
```

This implementation creates a `WordCounter` struct that stores the unique words and their frequency count. The `CountWords` method splits the input text into words using regular expressions and counts the frequency of each word. The `GetUniqueWordCount` and `GetWordFrequency` methods provide access to the word count information.

The main function sets up an HTTP server that listens for POST requests to the `/wordcount` endpoint. It reads the text from the request body, counts the words using the `WordCounter` instance, and returns the unique word count and frequency count as the response.

To handle large strings and concurrent requests efficiently, the implementation uses a mutex to synchronize access to the `words` map in the `WordCounter` struct. This ensures thread safety and prevents data races when multiple goroutines are processing requests concurrently.

The implementation also checks for the maximum length of the input string and returns appropriate HTTP error responses if the limit is exceeded. It handles case-insensitive word matching, punctuation and special characters, word boundaries, and different language-specific word delimiters using regular expressions.

To optimize memory usage, the implementation minimizes memory allocations by not storing duplicate words. Instead, it maintains a frequency count for each unique word. This reduces the memory footprint, especially for large strings with millions of words.

Finally, the implementation includes basic error handling and response formatting. It can be further enhanced with comprehensive unit tests to ensure correctness and performance under different scenarios.

